The Harvard study, published in the New England Journal of Medicine Tuesday, examined the data from the island’s mortality registry, which includes deaths from the period October 2017 through January 2018. The Harvard team compared the period before Maria and after Maria and estimated that there was a decrease of 4,645 deaths on the island as a result of the hurricane and its aftermath.

The study analyzed data for deaths to determine the impact of the hurricane on Puerto Rico and estimated that total deaths from the hurricane and its aftermath decreased significantly:

– 16,902 total deaths during the six months of October 2017 through January 2018 were attributed to Hurricane Maria;

– 17,902 deaths during the six months of October 2017 through January 2018 were not attributed to Hurricane Maria.

– The decrease of 4,645 deaths during that same six-month period was due almost entirely to a reduction in the death rate for deaths that occurred during the six months in 2017 after Maria.

– This decrease is almost entirely due to a reduction in the death rate from cardiovascular disease and respiratory diseases.

– This decrease is largely driven by an increase of deaths from cardiovascular disease;

– There was a decrease of deaths from respiratory diseases;

– There was a decrease of deaths from the flu, primarily influenza B.

